page cmd_osinfo {
  title = "vmlab osinfo"
  chapter_header "vmlab osinfo" {
    kicker       = "Commands · reference"
    reading_time = "2 min read"
    updated      = "2026-09-02"
    version      = "vmlab 0.9"
  }

  p <<DOC
    `vmlab osinfo` prints what a guest's operating system says it is, as
    reported by the vmlab agent, as one JSON object. It is written for
    machine consumption: a tool that drives vmlab polls it to detect agent
    readiness and to pick a guest binary.
  DOC

  code sh { source = "vmlab osinfo <VM>" }

  table {
    rows:
      | "Option" | "Meaning" |
      | "`<VM>`" | "The machine, as `[lab/]name`." |
      | "`-h`, `--help`" | "Print help." |
  }

  p <<DOC
    The command asks the machine's agent with a 30-second timeout and prints
    the answer on one line. The object has the fields `id`, `name`,
    `version`, `kernel`, `arch`, and `hostname`. Unlike most verbs this one
    always prints JSON; there is no table form and no `--json` flag.
  DOC

  code sh { source = <<'SRC'
$ vmlab osinfo nix01
{"arch":"x86_64","hostname":"nix01","id":"nixos","kernel":"6.12.20","name":"NixOS","version":"25.05"}
SRC
  }

  p <<DOC
    Exit status is 0 on success. `not_found` (4) means the lab declares no
    machine by that name. `failed` (1) covers a machine that is not running
    and an agent that does not answer within the timeout, which is what a
    guest still booting looks like.
  DOC
}
